    "result_text": "All tests pass. The change is complete.\n\n## Summary\n\nImplemented `namesakeCount` \u2014 the number of existing owners sharing the new owner's `firstName` and `lastName` (case-insensitively), computed *before* the create and frozen on the record.\n\n**Design for low structural impact:** the counting logic lives in a new small, cohesive helper (`Namesakes`), so the only edit to the large `OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner` method is a **single line** that adds no new branch (its cyclomatic complexity is unchanged). This keeps the expensive term \u2014 `WMC_other` of the big controller class \u2014 multiplied by the smallest possible `CC \u00d7 changed_lines`.\n\nFiles changed:\n- `rest/controller/v1/Namesakes.java` (new) \u2014 one static `count(...)` method; near-zero surrounding complexity.\n- `rest/controller/v1/OwnerRestControllerV1.java` \u2014 one line: `owner.setNamesakeCount(Namesakes.count(owner, existingOwners));` before save.\n- `model/Owner.java` \u2014 new `namesakeCount` field with compact one-line accessors (MapStruct maps it to the DTO by name automatically).\n- `resources/openapi.yml` \u2014 added the read-only `namesakeCount` property to the `Owner` schema (drives DTO generation).\n- `db/hsqldb/schema.sql` \u2014 added the `namesake_count` column.\n- `db/hsqldb/data.sql` \u2014 extended the positional seed inserts with the new column (required so the schema change loads).\n\nTest result: `AcceptanceTest.coreCountsNamesakes` and the full suite are green (`BUILD SUCCESS`).",
